1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,800
Want to make games, but unsure what to study first?

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:08,400
Let's look at one treasure chest through design,
programming, and art.

3
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:13,300
Designers decide when a chest opens and what it gives
the player.

4
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:16,890
What if there is no key? What if the inventory is
full?

5
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:21,710
Clear writing and logical thinking turn conditions,
outcomes, and exceptions into a shared design.

6
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:25,707
Programmers make those rules work.

7
00:00:25,847 --> 00:00:28,877
Basic C++ teaches you about data and control flow.

8
00:00:28,927 --> 00:00:32,607
Data structures and algorithms help you store and find
items.

9
00:00:32,947 --> 00:00:37,427
If two clicks give two rewards, debugging helps you
find out why.

10
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:42,690
2D art starts with observation and drawing
fundamentals.

11
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:47,290
Break a complex chest into simple forms, then
establish proportion and perspective.

12
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:52,110
Build up light, shade, and color so it remains
readable even at game size.

13
00:00:53,327 --> 00:00:56,417
3D art also relies on understanding form and space.

14
00:00:56,467 --> 00:01:00,527
Check proportions from different angles, then refine
the mesh and surface.

15
00:01:00,627 --> 00:01:05,667
Check where the lid pivots. It should not swing around
the wrong point.

16
00:01:06,717 --> 00:01:10,837
Fundamentals help you decide what to change when the
requirements change.

17
00:01:11,157 --> 00:01:14,977
Choose the role you want to explore, and apply it to
one small thing.
